#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218320 :tutorial/content "Consider the line\n\n$$\ny = x + 2\n$$\n\nand the parabola\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + x + 1\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations\n\n$$\ny = x + 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + x + 1\n$$\n\nIn other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = x^{2} + x + 1; y = x + 2\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + x + 1 &= x + 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + x + 1 &= x + 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + 1 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n$y|= x + 2; = (-1) + 2; = 1$,\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1)$.\n- Substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= x + 2; = (1) + 2; = 3$,\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\,3 \\right)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b907a9fb-d730-5d33-b47d-e448e4bc64dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13862 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218321 :tutorial/content "![](images/b9/b9cb25bbb7b3076a3dae8688a232d18ec4092d68d9e815dedb438bc6900d97bb.png)\n\nA line and a parabola may have **two**,\n**one**, or **no** points of intersection.\n\nThis is similar to how a quadratic equation may have **two**,\n**one**, or **no** solutions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "befa8bb3-607a-57e3-b906-28765ffb7779" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13863 :tutorial/title "The Number of Intersection Points"}]}
